{
  "dom_id": "step-e15615",
  "name": "Example: Finding the Midline of a Transformed Sinusoidal Function",
  "problem": "Find the equation of the midline for a transformed sine function with a minimum value of $-6$ and a maximum value of $8.$",
  "worked_solution": "A sinusoidal graph's midline (or vertical shift) is the horizontal line that divides it vertically in half. \n\nThe equation of the midline is given by\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{y_{\\textrm{min}}+y_{\\textrm{max}}}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $y_{\\textrm{min}}$ and $y_{\\textrm{max}}$ are the minimum and maximum values of the function. The midline equals the average of the maximum and minimum values. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2} & =\\frac{-6+8}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midline is $y=1.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
